        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Atlanta projects, intown and north</strong></p><p>Atlanta is hilly Piedmont country, and most intown lots slope. Bungalows in Morningside, Virginia-Highland, Candler Park and Decatur have back decks that stand a full story above a walk-out basement. A glass deck railing there keeps the yard and the tree canopy in view instead of a wall of pickets. Along the BeltLine, Inman Park and Old Fourth Ward add rooftop decks on new townhomes.</p><p>Midtown and Buckhead are condo territory, with balconies along Peachtree that need a 42-inch glass balcony railing. Buckhead, Brookhaven and Sandy Springs houses add pool decks and terraces above the Chattahoochee. Farther north, Alpharetta, Roswell, Johns Creek and Milton are full of two-story homes with open staircases. Builders there put glass stair railing on the main stair and a glass guard on the upstairs landing. Marietta and Dunwoody follow the same pattern.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Permits and the code in Atlanta</strong></p><p>Georgia applies the 2018 International Residential Code and International Building Code with Georgia amendments, under the State Minimum Standard Codes. The Georgia code sets guards at 36 inches minimum for houses and 42 inches for commercial and multifamily buildings, including condo balconies. For a pool deck, the 2018 International Swimming Pool and Spa Code, section 305, sets a 48-inch barrier. Our 46-inch panel in spigots gives that height.</p><p>Inside the city limits, the City of Atlanta Office of Buildings issues the permit. Sandy Springs, Brookhaven, Dunwoody, Decatur, Alpharetta and Roswell each have their own building office. Unincorporated addresses go through Fulton, DeKalb or Cobb County. Check with your local building department about whether a railing replacement or a new deck needs a permit. Our engineering documents are available for the file, and the <a href=\"/pages/georgia-building-code-for-glass-railing\">Georgia building code for glass railing</a> page has the full summary.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Built for Atlanta pollen, heat and storms</strong></p><p>Every March and April, pine pollen turns Atlanta yellow. It settles on decks, cars and railings for weeks. On glass it rinses off with a hose in minutes, with no balusters to scrub between. Summers run humid with highs near 90 degrees and afternoon thunderstorms. Wood railings on a south-facing deck gray out and need staining every couple of years. Tempered 12 mm glass and 2205 marine-grade stainless steel do not rot or rust, and there is nothing to stain.</p><p>Thunderstorms bring gusty wind and falling branches from Atlanta's tree canopy. The spigots are bolted through the deck boards into blocking, as the engineering documents show. The 12 mm panels are tempered safety glass, not window glass. Winters are mild, with an occasional ice storm. Freeze-thaw does not affect either material, so the railing looks the same in February as in July.</p>"
          }
        }
